# WindowMan UI/UX Rules

These rules apply whenever Cursor touches frontend UI, React components, pages, user flows, styling, accessibility, or Lovable-generated code.

UI is the presentation layer. It may display state, stage user input, and improve usability. It must not become the source of truth for security-sensitive, database-mutating, ownership, billing, upload-completion, scan/session, or irreversible workflow state.

---

## 1. Prime Directive

Never solve backend authority problems in the UI.

The UI must not be trusted for:

- user identity
- authorization
- ownership
- scan/session state
- upload completion
- final submission state
- billing/payment state
- irreversible workflow transitions

If UI behavior depends on missing backend data, do not fake it with local state. Route the issue to the protected service, RPC, Edge Function, or backend authority layer.

If frontend work touches Supabase, RLS, RPCs, Edge Functions, storage, or generated types, also inspect `.cursor/rules/supabase.mdc`.

---

## 2. Lovable Rules

Lovable is allowed to generate UI only.

Lovable-generated code must not define or invent:

- canonical business logic
- database writes
- fake RPC return shapes
- fake Supabase clients
- fake Twilio/OTP flows
- authorization logic
- ownership checks
- final workflow status
- service-role behavior
- production routing behavior

When importing or modifying Lovable output:

- preserve the visual idea only if it fits WindowMan
- strip hallucinated backend logic
- replace fake data with real typed props or existing service boundaries
- keep components thin
- move business rules out of the component
- verify accessibility
- verify responsive behavior
- verify no new dependency was added casually

Lovable is a visual drafting tool, not the system architect.

---

## 3. Visual Style

Default to restrained, premium, enterprise UI.

Use:

- native OS system font stack
- clear hierarchy
- accessible contrast
- precise spacing
- subtle depth
- restrained shadows
- crisp cards and panels
- deliberate empty/loading/error states
- minimal animation
- explicit user control

Preferred font stack:

```css
font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", system-ui, sans-serif;
Reject custom web fonts by default unless explicitly required for brand reasons.

Avoid:

noisy gradients
gratuitous glassmorphism
excessive skeuomorphism
novelty animations
overdesigned decorative elements
vague “AI startup” visual clutter
layout shifts caused by font or asset loading
4. User Flow Rules

For uploads, submissions, scans, payments, verification, or irreversible actions:

prefer staged flows
require explicit user action
avoid surprise auto-advance
show clear progress
show clear success and failure states
allow recovery where possible
prevent duplicate submissions in the UI
still rely on backend authority for final state
do not mark completion from client-only flags

File upload flows must separate:

file selected
file staged
user confirms
upload starts
upload succeeds or fails
backend verifies final state
UI reflects authoritative result

Do not auto-advance the funnel unless explicitly requested and verified as safe.

5. Component Boundaries

Keep UI components focused on presentation and interaction.

Components may handle:

local UI state
form input state
visual loading states
staged previews
local validation for user experience
calling existing service hooks/functions

Components must not contain:

canonical business authority
ownership decisions
permission checks as the only protection
direct privileged API calls
service-role usage
hardcoded fake backend results
duplicated RPC logic
large orchestration workflows

If logic grows large, extract it to the appropriate existing hook, service, route, protected service, or backend layer.

6. Frontend State Rules

Use frontend state for temporary UI concerns only.

Good frontend state:

modal open/closed
selected tab
staged file before upload
form field values
local validation messages
optimistic display only when safe
temporary preview state

Bad frontend state:

“this scan is complete”
“this upload is verified”
“this user owns this record”
“this payment is settled”
“this OTP is valid”
“this session is authorized”
“this backend mutation succeeded” without backend confirmation

Do not duplicate canonical server state across scattered components.

7. Accessibility Rules

Accessibility is required.

When editing UI, preserve or improve:

semantic HTML
keyboard navigation
visible focus states
form labels
accessible error messages
contrast
touch target size
reduced-motion compatibility
screen-reader-safe status changes
logical heading order
real buttons for actions
real links for navigation

Do not use div or span as buttons.

Do not remove focus outlines unless replacing them with visible accessible focus styles.

Do not rely on color alone to communicate state.

Do not add motion that ignores reduced-motion preferences.

8. Error, Loading, and Empty States

Every user-facing async flow should distinguish:

loading
empty
unauthorized
forbidden
not found
validation error
backend error
network error
success

Do not collapse everything into theatrical loading.

Do not hide backend errors behind indefinite spinners.

Do not show success until the backend/protected service confirms success.

For the scan flow specifically, avoid UI that stays permanently in “processing,” “analyzing,” “theatrics,” or similar visual states without exposing the actual failed dependency.

9. Dependency Rules

Do not add UI dependencies casually.

Before adding:

UI kits
icon libraries
animation libraries
form libraries
state managers
date libraries
styling systems

inspect existing dependencies and patterns.

Prefer existing stack and components.

Do not modify lockfiles unless a dependency change is explicitly justified.

Do not introduce heavy UI libraries for one component.

10. Responsive and Layout Rules

UI must work across reasonable viewport sizes.

Check:

mobile width
desktop width
long text
empty content
error content
loading skeletons
overflow behavior
scroll behavior
sticky/fixed elements
keyboard/focus behavior
modals/dialogs

Avoid layout that only works for the happy-path screenshot.

11. Performance Rules

Avoid frontend performance regressions.

Watch for:

unnecessary re-renders
repeated polling
duplicate network calls
oversized components
excessive client hydration
heavy animation
unbounded lists
large images/assets without sizing
importing server-only code into client components

Do not optimize prematurely, but do not introduce obvious waste.

12. Final Response Requirements

After UI/UX edits, Cursor must report:

files changed
why each file changed
whether any backend, auth, Supabase, Twilio, or routing behavior changed
accessibility checks performed
responsive checks performed
manual verification needed
unresolved risks

If the UI issue appears to be caused by backend/RPC/auth failure, stop and report that instead of masking it in the component.
